---
import Layout from "../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
const pageTitle = 'Welfare Unit Hire: Toilets, Canteens and Drying Rooms';
const pageDescription = 'Plan construction welfare unit hire with guidance on toilets, washing, rest areas, changing, drying, drinking water, capacity, utilities and positioning.';
const canonicalUrl = new URL(Astro.url.pathname, Astro.site).href;
const structuredData = {"@context": "https://schema.org", "@graph": [{"@type": "Article", "headline": "Welfare Unit Hire: Toilets, Canteens and Drying Rooms", "description": "Plan construction welfare unit hire with guidance on toilets, washing, rest areas, changing, drying, drinking water, capacity, utilities and positioning.", "mainEntityOfPage": canonicalUrl, "publisher": {"@type": "Organization", "name": "Portaloo Online", "url": new URL("/", Astro.site).href}}, {"@type": "FAQPage", "mainEntity": [{"@type": "Question", "name": "What is a construction welfare unit?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "A mobile or static unit containing some combination of toilets, washing, rest, eating, changing, storage and drying facilities."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Who is responsible for site welfare?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "HSE says contractors must provide suitable welfare, commercial clients must ensure arrangements exist, and principal contractors must ensure provision throughout the project."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Does a welfare unit need hot water?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Suitable washing facilities should include clean hot and cold, or warm, running water."}}, {"@type": "Question", "name": "Can one welfare unit serve a large site?", "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Not always. HSE says remote parts of larger sites may require additional toilets, washing facilities or satellite welfare."}}]}]};
---
<Layout title={`${pageTitle} | Portaloo Online`} description={pageDescription}>
<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(structuredData)} />
<main class="guide-page"><section class="guide-hero"><div class="container hero-container"><nav class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><a href="/guides/">Guides</a><span>/</span><span>Welfare Unit Hire: Toilets, Canteens and Drying Rooms</span></nav><p class="eyebrow">Construction welfare guide</p><h1>Welfare Unit Hire: Toilets, Canteens and Drying Rooms</h1><p class="hero-intro">Plan construction welfare unit hire with guidance on toilets, washing, rest areas, changing, drying, drinking water, capacity, utilities and positioning.</p><div class="guide-meta"><span>9 minute read</span><span>Updated October 2026</span></div></div></section><div class="container article-layout"><aside class="contents"><div class="contents-card"><p class="contents-title">In this guide</p><nav><a href="#quick-answer">Quick answer</a><a href="#requirements">Site requirements</a><a href="#types">Types of unit</a><a href="#facilities">Facilities</a><a href="#capacity">Choosing capacity</a><a href="#utilities">Power, water and waste</a><a href="#position">Positioning</a><a href="#checklist">Hire checklist</a><a href="#faqs">FAQs</a><a href="#find-suppliers">Find suppliers</a></nav></div></aside><article class="guide-content"><section class="summary-box" id="quick-answer"><h2>Quick answer</h2><p>Welfare units combine facilities needed by workers, commonly including toilets, washing, seating, eating space, heating and storage or drying provision. The required specification depends on the workforce and work activities.</p><ul><li>Plan welfare before work starts</li><li>Match capacity to worker numbers and shifts</li><li>Provide suitable toilets and washing</li><li>Provide rest and eating areas</li><li>Consider changing, storage, drying and showers</li><li>Maintain water, power, waste and servicing</li></ul></section><section id="requirements"><h2>What welfare must construction sites provide?</h2><p>HSE says everyone working on a construction site must have access to toilets and facilities for washing, changing, eating and rest. Contractors provide welfare, commercial clients ensure arrangements exist, and principal contractors ensure provision from the start to the end of a project.</p></section><section id="types"><h2>Types of welfare unit</h2><div class="card-grid"><article><h3>Towable units</h3><p>Mobile units for smaller or changing sites, often combining a toilet, seating and basic kitchen facilities.</p></article><article><h3>Static cabins</h3><p>Larger modular accommodation for longer projects and bigger workforces.</p></article><article><h3>Eco or solar units</h3><p>Units designed to reduce generator use, although utilities and operating limits still require checking.</p></article><article><h3>Specialist units</h3><p>Separate toilet, shower, drying, changing or canteen facilities where one combined unit is insufficient.</p></article></div></section><section id="facilities"><h2>Facilities to consider</h2><ul><li>Toilets and warm running water for washing</li><li>Soap and suitable hand-drying facilities</li><li>Tables and seats with backs</li><li>Facilities to prepare food and boil water</li><li>Heating in winter and ventilation in summer</li><li>Secure storage for clothing and personal effects</li><li>Drying space for wet workwear</li><li>Drinking water</li><li>Showers where the work requires them</li></ul></section><section id="capacity"><h2>Choosing capacity</h2><p>Use the maximum number of workers likely to use the unit at one time, including overlapping shifts. Check the number of seats, toilet capacity, water storage, waste capacity and drying space rather than relying only on the unit's marketing name.</p></section><section id="utilities"><h2>Power, water and waste</h2><p>Confirm how heating, hot water, lighting and appliances are powered. Check fresh-water filling, waste-tank emptying, generator servicing and fuel arrangements.</p><ul><li>Electrical connection or generator</li><li>Fresh-water supply or tank</li><li>Waste storage and emptying</li><li>Heating and ventilation</li><li>Lighting and charging points</li><li>Service access throughout the project</li></ul></section><section id="position"><h2>Positioning the unit</h2><p>HSE says facilities should be quickly accessible and may need moving as work progresses. Remote areas of large sites may need additional or satellite welfare.</p><ul><li>Use stable ground and safe access</li><li>Keep the distance from work areas short</li><li>Protect the facility from site traffic</li><li>Maintain cleaning and maintenance access</li><li>Review placement as the project changes</li></ul></section><section id="checklist"><h2>Welfare unit hire checklist</h2><div class="checklist"><label><input type="checkbox" /> Maximum workforce and shifts confirmed</label><label><input type="checkbox" /> Toilet and washing capacity checked</label><label><input type="checkbox" /> Seating and eating space checked</label><label><input type="checkbox" /> Changing, storage and drying needs assessed</label><label><input type="checkbox" /> Power and fuel arrangements agreed</label><label><input type="checkbox" /> Water and waste servicing agreed</label><label><input type="checkbox" /> Delivery and ground conditions checked</label><label><input type="checkbox" /> Cleaning responsibility assigned</label></div></section><section id="faqs"><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="faq-list"><details><summary>What is a construction welfare unit?</summary><p>A mobile or static unit containing some combination of toilets, washing, rest, eating, changing, storage and drying facilities.</p></details><details><summary>Who is responsible for site welfare?</summary><p>HSE says contractors must provide suitable welfare, commercial clients must ensure arrangements exist, and principal contractors must ensure provision throughout the project.</p></details><details><summary>Does a welfare unit need hot water?</summary><p>Suitable washing facilities should include clean hot and cold, or warm, running water.</p></details><details><summary>Can one welfare unit serve a large site?</summary><p>Not always. HSE says remote parts of larger sites may require additional toilets, washing facilities or satellite welfare.</p></details></div></section><section class="sources-box"><h2>Sources and further guidance</h2><ul><li><a href="https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/healthrisks/welfare/index.htm" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer">HSE construction welfare overview</a></li><li><a href="https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/healthrisks/welfare/toilets-and-washing.htm" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer">HSE toilets and washing</a></li><li><a href="https://www.hse.gov.uk/construction/healthrisks/welfare/changing-rooms-and-lockers.htm" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer">HSE changing, eating and rest areas</a></li></ul></section><section class="find-suppliers" id="find-suppliers"><h2>Find suitable hire companies</h2><p>Browse portable toilet and temporary welfare suppliers serving towns and cities across the UK.</p><a class="primary-button" href="/locations/">Browse toilet hire locations</a></section><section class="disclaimer"><h2>Important</h2><p>This guide provides general planning information. Confirm the final specification, utilities, access, servicing and compliance requirements with an experienced supplier and the relevant authority.</p></section></article></div></main></Layout>
<style>
